WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.930
Film Courage: In all of your classes (growing
up) was there one instructor that said something

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:09.340
to you which really changed your way of thinking
about writing?

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:11.679
And do you remember that moment and what it
was?

00:00:11.679 --> 00:00:13.570
Where it really popped, it made sense?

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:16.320
It could be something very basic or more elaborate?

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.270
CSUN Professor Eric Edson: Hmmmmm.

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:21.920
Film Courage: Whether you were sitting in
class, whether it was notes they gave you?

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:27.210
Eric Edson: One of the things about being
at the MFA program in playwriting (this was,

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:40.790
so it was fairly early on for me at UCLA),
they…as all universities they are very big

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:49.510
on employing, inviting in as lecturers and
it’s part-time people known and greatly

00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:52.770
experienced in the industries.

00:00:52.770 --> 00:01:00.370
And they would come in and teach a course,
for instance a graduate course.

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:06.210
There was a world-famous playwright in particular
who came in and did this course.

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:15.350
A wonderful, wonderful man and a major talent
and I took the course and it wasn’t the

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:20.440
ultimate word of wisdom, that’s not what
I took away.

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:31.310
What I took away from that course is every
writer and every life is different and in

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:39.020
a way sometimes that we do not even want to
hear, it is truly up to us.

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:45.570
Writers have in their core hopefully the ability
you know we’ve been alone all of our lives

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:53.220
and our thoughts to stand alone because hearing
about other people’s journeys in a personal

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:58.670
way, these people just weren’t (I mean this
particular individual too was a wonderful

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:01.540
soul).

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:06.530
But basically what he did was he told old
war stories all semester long.

00:02:06.530 --> 00:02:10.660
And what we were writing, we wrote on our
own, it was up to us.

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:23.030
In the entire time that I was in UCLA, nobody
made any serious attempt to teach me the toolkit

00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:28.730
or approach or structure of writing full-length
plays.

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:33.510
It was all touchy-feely and all up to you.

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:37.530
And for awhile there I was a bit upset about
that, a bit ticked about that.

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:40.170
It did cost money and all that stuff.

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:49.819
But yes, I learned what they didn’t perhaps
intend to teach which is one person’s journey

00:02:49.819 --> 00:02:51.990
is going to be different from yours.

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:57.750
Now this is kind of the opposite from what
you were asking for, you know this ultimate

00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:00.500
gem or jewel of wisdom.

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:08.050
But I think it is a jewel of wisdom that these
are good, brilliant, accomplished artists

00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:16.001
who are most sincere in their ability to share
how they’ve done it and what they did, be

00:03:16.001 --> 00:03:24.130
able to share that with these up-and-coming
writers before them in the grant program.

00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:32.110
But I came away feeling they didn’t have
a whole lot to share and that’s okay, I

00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:33.379
get that.

00:03:33.379 --> 00:03:34.610
We will make our own path.

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:40.370
Film Courage: Do you think because they were
so busy doing that they had a hard time articulating

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:42.610
how they did it or no?

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:45.150
Eric Edson: I don’t know.

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:52.810
I think most of them were instinctive writers,
they didn’t codify it.

00:03:52.810 --> 00:03:58.120
And that’s why when I later came to teach
screenplays, I was kind of obsessed with this

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:00.300
has to be…this is teachable.

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:01.300
These are smart people.

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:05.010
They can learn if we can make this teachable,
it can be taught.

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:11.670
I mean there are certain levels at which skill,
talent (it helps to have talent).

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:16.440
But we all know (I certainly think), I hope
most people understand that it’s not necessarily

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:22.500
the most talented writers who end up getting
their scripts sold and even made.

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:24.449
That’s not how it works.

00:04:24.449 --> 00:04:27.229
They come up with ideas for stories.

00:04:27.229 --> 00:04:33.240
Hollywood buys stories, not particular scripts
by particular writers.

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:38.639
Film Courage: And they are stories that take
people on a specific emotional journey knowing

00:04:38.639 --> 00:04:42.419
most likely that they are going to come out
feeling good at the end.

00:04:42.419 --> 00:04:44.360
Eric Edson: Yes.

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:49.009
Less so with feeling good at the end now today
then Once Upon a Time.

00:04:49.009 --> 00:04:50.539
Have you seen LIFE?

00:04:50.539 --> 00:04:52.880
We just saw that recently, have you seen LIFE?

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:54.680
Film Courage: Not yet.

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:56.030
Eric Edson: Whoah!

00:04:56.030 --> 00:04:57.569
Speaking of a negative ending.

00:04:57.569 --> 00:05:01.350
Film Courage: Yeah…but there are still the
gems.

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:05.710
I felt like after September things changed
but that’s a whole other topic.

00:05:05.710 --> 00:05:07.009
Eric Edson: Yeah, good point.

